{
  "image_name": "0c47332b65b879b5ea339eeb3b30233c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how innate immune receptors recognize oxidized or damage-related molecules on dying cells, microvesicles, and oxidized LDL, and how this drives either inflammation, uptake, or clearance. The damaged surface is shown carrying several oxidation-specific epitopes, including PC-OxPL, MDA, 4-HNE, CEP, and OxPS.\n\nA first group of membrane-bound pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs) detects these altered lipids. Toll-like receptors, often shown together with CD36, as well as receptors such as SRB1, LOX1, SRA1, and CD36, mediate inflammatory signaling and/or uptake of the damaged material. Some receptors mainly link to inflammation, others to uptake, and some do both.\n\nA second group consists of soluble PRRs and opsonins that promote noninflammatory disposal. Molecules such as MFG-E8, C1q, CRP, IgM, MBL, complement factor H, and iC3b bind the oxidized surface and connect it to phagocytic receptors including αvβ3 integrin, CR3, calreticulin/CD91, and the MBL receptor. These interactions are labeled largely as leading to “clearance.”\n\nOverall, the image contrasts two linked outcomes of recognizing oxidation-specific danger signals: proinflammatory cellular sensing versus opsonin-assisted engulfment and removal of dying or oxidized particles.",
